atDirectives.description:
You can generate responsive variants of your own classes by wrapping their definitions in the `@responsive` directive:
```css
@responsive {
  .alert {
    background-color: #E53E3E;
  }
}
```

The `@screen` directive allows you to create media queries that reference your breakpoints by **name** instead of duplicating their values in your own CSS:
```css
@screen sm {
  /* ... */
}
```
…gets transformed into this:
```css
@media (min-width: 640px) {
  /* ... */
}
```

Generate `hover`, `focus`, `active` and other **variants** of your own utilities by wrapping their definitions in the `@variants` directive:
```css
@variants hover, focus {
   .btn-brand {
    background-color: #3182CE;
  }
}
```

reference other CSS files in your project to make their classes available for `@apply` and `@variants` directives. This is useful when you want to create custom utility classes that are based on your own CSS instead of Tailwind's built-in utilities:
```css
@reference "./components.css";
```

The `@theme` directive allows you to access values from your Tailwind config file in your CSS. This is useful when you want to create custom utility classes that are based on your own design tokens instead of Tailwind's built-in utilities:
```css
.btn {
  color: theme('colors.blue.500');
}
```
